Dear all,

I have run a confirmatory factor analysis on 11 dichotomous manifest variables, with standardised latent variables, theta parameterisation and the WLSMV estimator (sample size ~ 5000). The best-fitting model supports a two, correlated latent variable measurement model. I am interested in whether certain manifest variables have residual correlations with each other, to test the assumption of local independence (my hypothesis is that there are violations of local independence between a prior defined indicators).

Fitting a model that tests certain residual correlations converges fine and marginally improves the fit indices (CFI, TLI, RMSEA), but a few of the residual correlations have 95% confidence intervals that exceed the interval [-1, 1]. All except one are the residual correlations between manifest variables loading onto the same latent variable. I have not attempted to interpret these correlations, since they may indicate inadequacy in estimation, but how much should one trust the residual correlations falling within the interval [-1, 1].  Is the model inadequate, in general? Unfortunately, I cannot share the data publicly.

Best
Conor

