MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> a few of the residual correlations have 95% confidence intervals that exceed the interval [-1, 1].

You are using theta parameterization, so the residuals have variances fixed to 1.  Thus, the residual covariances are thus not bound between [-1, 1].  If you want to see them in a correlation metric, you can request "std = TRUE" in the summary() output or the parameterEstimates() output, or just by using the standardizedSolution() function.  None of these methods provide a CI for the correlation estimate. Using the SE from standardizedSolution() is not appropriate because it assumes a normal (symmetric) sampling distribution that doesn't recognize the [-1, 1] boundary.  You could use the bootstrapLavaan() function if you want CIs around an effect size estimate, but you can use the unstandardized solution for the null hypothesis of zero because a correlation == 0 corresponds to a covariance == 0.

Terrence D. Jorgensen
